# Supercronic crontab.
#
# Active schedules are uncommented below; commented lines are examples. Edit
# this file on the host, then restart the container to pick it up.
#
# Format: one line per schedule.
#   <five cron fields>  /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh [-t <name>] "<prompt>"
#
# -t names a destination in NOTIFY_TARGETS. The name must exist there; an
# unknown name fails before sending. A target with a host_scope also tells the
# agent which host the message may talk about, and allow_content false blocks
# the content-search tool for that run. Omit -t to use NOTIFY_DEFAULT_TARGET,
# or the legacy COMPOSIO_ARGS configuration when no default is set.
#
# The five fields are minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week.
# Times use the container's TZ (set TZ in stack.env; default is UTC).
# Only edit this file and rebuild the image: it is baked in read-only at /app.
# Schedules are trusted operator configuration, not user input.
#
# Examples (uncomment one line per schedule):
#
# Daily digest at 09:00.
# 0 9 * * * /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh "Compose a short daily digest for the team. Use the heading 'Daily digest', list the standing items: review the board, check overnight alerts, and note any blockers. End with a reminder to update the board. Do not invent news or metrics. Send it now with notify.sh."
#
# Morning host + SSH digest at 09:00, to the automations target. This keeps the
# old host-report shape and adds SSH status. The target's host_scope must
# include rossmoney.me, photos.rossmoney.me and vortexservers.co.uk.
0 9 * * * NOTIFY_PREFETCH=host_stats NOTIFY_PREFETCH_WINDOW_MINUTES=1440 /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh -t automations "Compose the morning digest. The 24-hour host statistics are supplied in this instruction; use them and do not call oracle_query for host statistics. Then call ssh_probe for SSH status. Use the old host report shape: one line per host with sample count, last-seen age, the CPU core count ('2 cores' / '1 core', or 'cores n/a' when the count is null), CPU average and peak, memory average, load average and peak, disk from the latest sample, and failed logins from the latest sample, plus SSH success with latency or the classified failure. Write failed logins as the count and then the top three usernames with their counts, for example 'failed logins: 12 (root 9, admin 3)'; when the count is null write 'failed logins: n/a', and when it is zero write 'failed logins: 0'. Never invent a username or a number that is not in the data. ssh_probe answers reachability and key authentication only and must not be used as the source of failed-login figures: those figures come from the supplied statistics. Then state the accepted logins from the latest sample as 'logins: 12 last hour (root 9, ross 3), 497 today': the last-hour count, then the top three usernames by count in that last-hour list, then the running UTC-day total; when a last-hour figure is null write 'logins: n/a', and when the day's total is null write 'today: n/a'. Never invent a number or a username that is not in the data. Include every known host in this run, in order. Flag stale hosts (no sample in 20 minutes) and, for the rest, only sustained signals: average CPU >= 60, average memory >= 85, disk >= 85. Decide every flag from the averages: report CPU and load peaks as context only, and never flag a host because of a peak. Keep it under 2,000 characters; drop whole host lines from the end and say how many were not shown. Never mention a host outside this run's scope. If SSH is not configured, say so once. Call notify.sh only when at least one host is flagged; if none is, do not call notify.sh and do not reply: end the run silently with no output. notify.sh is the only way this run may say anything: never write a chat reply instead of, or in addition to, a notification. When a host is flagged, the notification itself carries the finding; there is no separate reply."
#
# Morning Vortex-only digest at 09:00, to the Vortex admin-chat target. The
# target's host_scope must be vortexservers.co.uk only.
# 0 9 * * * NOTIFY_PREFETCH=host_stats NOTIFY_PREFETCH_WINDOW_MINUTES=1440 /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh -t admin-chat "Compose the morning Vortex digest. The 24-hour host statistics are supplied in this instruction; use them and do not call oracle_query for host statistics. Then call ssh_probe for SSH status, for the Vortex hosts only. Use the old host report shape: one line per host with sample count, last-seen age, CPU average and peak, memory average, load average and peak, and disk from the latest sample, plus SSH success with latency or the classified failure. Include every known host in this run, in order. Flag stale hosts (no sample in 20 minutes) and the thresholds. Keep it under 2,000 characters; drop whole host lines from the end and say how many were not shown. Never mention a rossmoney host or anything about it. If SSH is not configured, say so once. Send it now with notify.sh."
#
# Weekdays at 17:30 - end-of-day wrap-up, explicitly to the "general" target.
# 30 17 * * 1-5 /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh -t general "Compose a brief end-of-day wrap-up asking the team to close out their tasks and flag anything that needs attention tomorrow. Send it now with notify.sh."
#
# Every Monday at 08:30 - weekly planning reminder.
# 30 8 * * 1 /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh "Compose a short Monday planning reminder: pick the top three priorities for the week and post them in the channel. Keep it under 300 characters. Send it now with notify.sh."
#
# First day of each month at 10:00 - monthly maintenance note.
# 0 10 1 * * /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh "Compose a short monthly maintenance reminder to rotate credentials and review notification schedules. Send it now with notify.sh."
#
# Every hour at minute 5 - heartbeat (noisy; useful only while testing).
# 5 * * * * /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh "Compose a one-line heartbeat message saying the notification agent is running. Send it now with notify.sh."

# Hourly host digest for the Vortex host, in the same shape as the automations digest below.
5 * * * * NOTIFY_PREFETCH=host_stats /app/scripts/run-prompt.sh -t admin-chat "The host statistics for the last 70 minutes are supplied in this instruction; use them and do not call oracle_query for host statistics. Probe SSH for each host. Flag a host ONLY when one of these holds: average CPU >= 60 percent; average memory >= 85 percent; disk >= 85 percent. Decide every flag from the averages: report CPU and load peaks as context only, and never flag a host because of a peak. State the sample count and last-seen age per host. Never mention a host outside this run's scope. If nothing is flagged, do not call notify.sh and do not reply: end the run silently with no output. notify.sh is the only way this run may say anything: never write a chat reply instead of, or in addition to, a notification. When a host is flagged, the notification itself carries the finding; there is no separate reply. Never invent or round a figure you did not read."

# Hourly host digest. Flags only on sustained signals; peaks are context, never a flag.
